[user] Thanks for the nudge. Yes—the legacy Gradebook tool ID (sakai.gradebook.tool) is still relevant in import scenarios, especially for older/archived sites and mixed deployments. Since getSiteImportToolsWithContent() already treats classic Gradebook and GradebookNG as equivalent, the selectable-content path should mirror that for consistent expand/collapse affordances.

Here’s a focused update to getSiteImportToolsWithSelectableContent() that restores Gradebook parity by checking both classic and NG when the requested toolId corresponds to Gradebook:

```java
// Replace the body of getSiteImportToolsWithSelectableContent with this version
private Map<String, Set<String>> getSiteImportToolsWithSelectableContent(List<Site> sites, List<String> toolIds) {
    Map<String, Set<String>> siteToolsWithSelectableContent = new HashMap<>();
    for (Site site : sites) {
        Set<String> toolsWithSelectableContent = new HashSet<>();
        for (String toolId : toolIds) {
            boolean hasSelectable = toolHasSelectableContent(toolId, site.getId());

            // Gradebook parity: if this request is for Gradebook, also check the other variant
            if (!hasSelectable
                && StringUtils.isNotBlank(toolId)
                && toolId.contains(GRADEBOOK_TOOL)) {
                hasSelectable =
                    toolHasSelectableContent(GRADEBOOK_TOOL_ID, site.getId()) ||
                    toolHasSelectableContent(GRADEBOOKNG_TOOL_ID, site.getId());
            }

            if (hasSelectable) {
                toolsWithSelectableContent.add(toolId);
            }
        }

        log.debug("Site: {}, has the following tools with selectable content: {}", site.getId(), toolsWithSelectableContent);
        siteToolsWithSelectableContent.put(site.getId(), toolsWithSelectableContent);
    }
    return siteToolsWithSelectableContent;
}
```

This mirrors the Gradebook handling already present in getSiteImportToolsWithContent(), so the UI’s expand/collapse control will appear consistently whether the source site uses classic Gradebook or GradebookNG.

