🌐 Web query:

```web
HttpServletRequest.getContentType() null behavior Java
```

💡 Result:

Short answer: getContentType() returns null when the request has no known MIME type (i.e., there is no Content-Type header or the container can't determine it). Use a null-safe check (and string contains/prefix check for multipart) instead of assuming a non-null value. [1][2]

Example pattern:
if (request.getContentType() != null && request.getContentType().toLowerCase().contains("multipart/form-data")) { ... }

Sources:
[1] ServletRequest.getContentType() Javadoc — "Returns the MIME type ... or null if the type is not known."  
[2] Common guidance (StackOverflow) — request.getContentType() may be null; check for null and use contains/startsWith for multipart/form-data.

---

🌐 Web query:

```web
OAuth 1.0a RFC content type requirements
```

💡 Result:

Short answer — OAuth 1.0a (RFC 5849) requires form-url-encoded bodies for OAuth protocol parameters:

- Request body parameters are only included in the signature/source if all of these hold: the entity is single-part, it follows the encoding rules of application/x-www-form-urlencoded, and the Content-Type header is set to application/x-www-form-urlencoded. (RFC 5849 §3.4.1.3). [1]

- Token responses (request_token / access_token) are returned in the HTTP response body using the application/x-www-form-urlencoded content type. (RFC 5849 §2 and §2.1 examples / §3.4.1.3). [1][2]

Implication: do not treat JSON or multipart/form-data bodies as OAuth parameter sources unless they are encoded and labelled as application/x-www-form-urlencoded. [1]

Sources:
[1] RFC 5849 (The OAuth 1.0 Protocol), see §3.4.1.3 and examples.  
[2] RFC 5849 (token response examples showing Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded).

