🌐 Web query:

`URLDecoder.decode deprecated Java what charset UTF-8`

💡 Result:

Yes — URLDecoder.decode(String) is deprecated because it uses the platform default charset (which is unsafe/ambiguous). Use one of the overloads that let you specify the encoding instead:

- URLDecoder.decode(String, Charset) or URLDecoder.decode(String, String) (e.g. "UTF-8"). [1][2]  
- The recommended charset for URL encoding/decoding is UTF-8 (W3C / Java docs). [2][3]

Example:
- URLDecoder.decode(s, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

References: [1] Java URLDecoder API (showing deprecated decode(String)). [2] Java URLEncoder/URLDecoder docs recommending UTF‑8. [3] JDK docs for decode(String, Charset).

---

🌐 Web query:

`MimeType constructor null handling javax.activation`

💡 Result:

Short answer: don’t pass null — the javax.activation.MimeType constructors declare they throw MimeTypeParseException for invalid input (and behavior for null isn't documented), so you must check for null before calling. Official Javadoc for MimeType(String) / MimeType(String primary, String sub) documents the exception but not null handling. [1][2]

Recommendation: guard like
if (s == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("mime type is null");
before calling new MimeType(s). [1][2]

Sources:
- javax.activation.MimeType (constructor docs). [1]
- javax.activation.MimeType (constructor docs, overload). [2]

